/// polymorph:dioxus — a batched DOM-mutation surface for Dioxus apps running
/// as components on polyengine.
///
/// Design: the guest queues DOM mutations into a compact byte format
/// (sledgehammer-bindgen-shaped) and delivers whole batches across the
/// boundary; the host decodes with a tight switch loop and applies them to a
/// live DOM subtree. Strings cross the boundary two ways: `&'static` names
/// (tags, attribute names, event names) are interned once and referenced by
/// u16 id thereafter; dynamic text is concatenated per batch and decoded with
/// a single TextDecoder pass.
///
/// The wire format below is normative and versions with this WIT package:
/// any change to opcodes, operand layouts, or framing bumps the package
/// version (instantiation is digest-checked, so a mismatch fails loudly and
/// early).
package polymorph:dioxus@0.1.0;

/// The mutation channel and the live-event handle.
///
/// # Transport
///
/// The guest creates a `stream<u8>` and hands the host the read end once,
/// before the first batch (`open`). The host parks a direct-read session on
/// it (polyengine amendment A21 `readDirect`): the decode callback runs
/// synchronously inside the guest's `stream.write` rendezvous over a view
/// aliasing guest linear memory — zero copies. Batches are framed (see
/// "Framing") because stream chunking is not preserved end-to-end.
///
/// (A second transport — one synchronous `flush(ops: list<u8>, strings:
/// string)` import call per batch — existed early in this package's
/// history as a benchmark baseline and debugging path. Retired after
/// bench/README.md's A/B: bulk-op deltas were within noise and the stream
/// transport was never meaningfully slower, while the call transport had
/// structurally degraded async semantics — see `run`'s doc below.)
///
/// # Batch layout
///
/// A batch has two segments:
///
/// - **string segment**: UTF-8 bytes, the concatenation of every dynamic
///   string operand (`dynstr` below) in op order. Decoded host-side in one
///   pass (`TextDecoder("utf-8", { ignoreBOM: true })` — BOM stripping must
///   be disabled or a leading U+FEFF in the first string would misalign
///   slicing), then sliced per-operand by UTF-16 code-unit lengths carried
///   in the op segment.
/// - **op segment**: opcodes and operands, little-endian, unaligned.
///
/// # Framing
///
/// ```text
/// frame     := frame-len:u32 strings-len:u32 strings:u8{strings-len} ops:u8{rest}
/// frame-len  = byte length of everything after the frame-len field
///            = 4 + strings-len + len(ops)
/// ```
///
/// Frames arrive back-to-back; a delivery may contain several frames or a
/// partial one (stream writers chunk internally). The decoder consumes only
/// whole frames from a direct-read view and leaves a partial tail unread
/// (redelivered at the next rendezvous); if it cannot consume at least one
/// byte it stages the partial tail instead (a direct-read callback must not
/// acknowledge zero bytes and keep the session parked).
///
/// # Primitive operand encodings
///
/// ```text
/// u8/u16/u32/s64/f64 : little-endian, unaligned
/// id     : u32   — Dioxus ElementId (slab index; dense). id 0 is the mount
///                  root: the host-designated element the app renders into.
/// m      : u32   — "top m nodes of the stack" count
/// tmpl   : u16   — guest-assigned template id
/// path   : u8 length, then that many u8 child indices
/// strref : u16   — interned string id, defined by a prior cache-string op
///                  (in this or any earlier batch). The value 0xffff is a
///                  "none" sentinel admitted ONLY by namespace operands
///                  (every `ns:strref` below, including template element and
///                  template attribute namespaces); tag/name operands are
///                  mandatory ids and never carry the sentinel.
/// dynstr : u16 UTF-16 code-unit length, then (iff 0xffff) u32 actual
///          length. Content is the next `length` code units of the decoded
///          string segment, consumed sequentially. The encoder appends the
///          string's UTF-8 bytes to the string segment and writes its
///          UTF-16 code-unit count here (Rust: sum of char::len_utf16,
///          with an all-ASCII fast path where it equals the byte length).
/// ```
///
/// # Opcodes
///
/// The vocabulary mirrors dioxus-core 0.7's `WriteMutations` trait plus two
/// protocol ops (cache-string, register-template). Stack semantics ("push",
/// "top m nodes") are Dioxus's mutation-stack semantics, identical to its
/// own web interpreter.
///
/// ```text
/// 0x01 cache-string        id:u16 s:dynstr
///        Define (or overwrite) interned slot `id`. Slots are guest-managed;
///        ids are assigned monotonically from 0 in practice.
/// 0x02 register-template   tmpl:u16 nroots:u16 root:node{nroots}
///        node := kind:u8 ...
///          0x00 element  tag:strref ns:strref nattrs:u16
///                        attr{nattrs} := name:strref ns:strref value:dynstr
///                        nchildren:u16 node{nchildren}
///          0x01 text     value:dynstr
///          0x02 dynamic  (placeholder for a runtime-supplied node)
///        Registers a static template; the host materializes it once
///        (detached) and later load-template clones root subtrees from it.
///        Guest sends each distinct template once, before its first use
///        (templates are 'static with unique identity, keyed guest-side by
///        pointer).
/// 0x03 append-children     id m
/// 0x04 assign-id           path id
/// 0x05 create-placeholder  id
/// 0x06 create-text-node    id text:dynstr
/// 0x07 load-template       tmpl root-index:u16 id
/// 0x08 replace-with        id m
/// 0x09 replace-placeholder path m
/// 0x0a insert-after        id m
/// 0x0b insert-before       id m
/// 0x0c set-attribute       id name:strref ns:strref value:attrval
///        attrval := kind:u8 ...
///          0x00 text   s:dynstr
///          0x01 float  f64
///          0x02 int    s64
///          0x03 bool   u8
///          0x04 none   (remove the attribute unconditionally — routed via
///                       removeAttribute/removeAttributeNS/style.removeProperty
///                       as the name/namespace dictates; the boolean-attribute
///                       truthiness table governs only text/bool VALUES.
///                       Property-backed fields get the same reset the
///                       truthy-value path uses instead of a bare
///                       removeAttribute, since removeAttribute alone does not
///                       clear a live element's property: value -> "" (+
///                       removeAttribute), checked -> false, selected ->
///                       false, dangerous_inner_html -> innerHTML = "".
///                       Ported from dioxus v0.7.10's `remove_attribute`
///                       sledgehammer op in
///                       packages/interpreter/src/unified_bindings.rs.)
///        The host applies Dioxus's attribute semantics (value/checked/
///        selected as properties, style namespace via style object,
///        dangerous_inner_html, boolean attribute removal on false).
/// 0x0d set-text            id text:dynstr
/// 0x0e new-event-listener  id name:strref flags:u8
/// 0x0f remove-event-listener id name:strref flags:u8
///        flags bit0 = the event bubbles (dioxus-html's event_bubbles table,
///        looked up guest-side): the host delegates bubbling events at the
///        mount root and attaches non-bubbling listeners per element, so it
///        needs the same bit on removal to find the registration. Bits 1..7
///        reserved, must be 0.
/// 0x10 remove              id
/// 0x11 push-root           id
/// ```
///
/// Event names cross back on `handle-event` as the same interned u16, so
/// steady-state event dispatch transfers no string data at all.
interface surface {
  /// Hand the host the read end of the mutation stream. Called at most
  /// once, before the first batch is written. The host keeps a
  /// direct-read session parked on it for the life of the instance.
  open: async func(ops: stream<u8>);

  /// A live DOM event, lent to the guest for the duration of one
  /// `handle-event` call (the cancelable window). Calling either method
  /// after the originating dispatch has completed is a harmless no-op
  /// (relevant when the guest was busy and the event was queued).
  resource dom-event {
    /// Call the native `preventDefault()`. The renderer invokes this
    /// synchronously (still inside the host's DOM listener frame) when the
    /// app's handler called `Event::prevent_default()` — Dioxus semantics
    /// require that flag to be set before the handler's first await.
    prevent-default: func();

    /// Call the native `stopPropagation()`. Dioxus bubbling is synthetic
    /// (guest-side); this affects only non-Dioxus listeners on the page.
    stop-propagation: func();
  }
}

/// Typed event payloads, host-serialized at dispatch. One snapshot per
/// event; fields not applicable to an event family are simply absent by
/// construction (the family is chosen host-side by event name, mirroring
/// dioxus-html's name→data-type mapping). Families not yet covered
/// (drag/touch/composition/animation/media specifics, onmounted) dispatch
/// as `empty` in this version.
interface events {
  flags modifiers { alt, ctrl, meta, shift }

  record mouse-data {
    client-x: f64,
    client-y: f64,
    page-x: f64,
    page-y: f64,
    screen-x: f64,
    screen-y: f64,
    offset-x: f64,
    offset-y: f64,
    /// Which button changed state (MouseEvent.button; -1 when not applicable).
    button: s16,
    /// Currently depressed buttons bitmask (MouseEvent.buttons).
    buttons: u16,
    mods: modifiers,
  }

  record pointer-data {
    mouse: mouse-data,
    pointer-id: s32,
    width: f64,
    height: f64,
    pressure: f64,
    tangential-pressure: f64,
    tilt-x: f64,
    tilt-y: f64,
    twist: f64,
    /// "mouse" | "pen" | "touch"
    pointer-type: string,
    is-primary: bool,
  }

  record keyboard-data {
    key: string,
    code: string,
    /// KeyboardEvent.location (0 standard, 1 left, 2 right, 3 numpad).
    location: u8,
    repeat: bool,
    is-composing: bool,
    mods: modifiers,
  }

  record wheel-data {
    mouse: mouse-data,
    delta-x: f64,
    delta-y: f64,
    delta-z: f64,
    /// WheelEvent.deltaMode (0 pixel, 1 line, 2 page).
    delta-mode: u8,
  }

  record form-data {
    /// The control's current value (input/textarea/select) or "" when not
    /// applicable.
    value: string,
    /// Present for checkable controls.
    checked: option<bool>,
    /// Form entries, populated for submit-shaped events on a form element
    /// (name → values, multi-valued controls preserved).
    values: list<tuple<string, list<string>>>,
  }

  record scroll-data {
    scroll-top: f64,
    scroll-left: f64,
    scroll-width: f64,
    scroll-height: f64,
    client-width: f64,
    client-height: f64,
  }

  variant payload {
    /// Events with no serialized data (focus/blur, load/error, select,
    /// toggle, click on non-interactive targets never carries less than
    /// mouse — see family mapping in the host).
    empty,
    mouse(mouse-data),
    pointer(pointer-data),
    keyboard(keyboard-data),
    wheel(wheel-data),
    form(form-data),
    scroll(scroll-data),
  }
}

/// A Dioxus application component. The host instantiates it, calls `run`
/// once (a long-lived task: it opens the mutation channel, mounts the app,
/// then loops on Dioxus's scheduler — driving `use_future`s and flushing
/// re-renders until the instance is torn down; its promise never settles in
/// normal operation), and dispatches DOM events via `handle-event`.
world app {
  use events.{payload};
  use surface.{dom-event};

  import surface;

  /// Start the app: open the mutation channel, perform the initial mount
  /// (rebuild → one batch), then serve the Dioxus scheduler forever. The
  /// persistent park between renders (a plain Rust future woken cross-task,
  /// no WIT waitable) is legal because the host retains a parked
  /// direct-read session on the guest's ops stream — component-model-async
  /// amendment A15 host retention — so a quiescent instance is the
  /// documented embedder-may-act state.
  export run: async func();

  /// Dispatch one DOM event to the listener registered on `target` (the
  /// ElementId carried by new-event-listener) for the interned event name
  /// `name`. The guest runs matching handlers synchronously (Dioxus
  /// synthetic bubbling included) and, if a handler prevented the default,
  /// calls `ev.prevent-default()` before returning. Renders triggered by
  /// the event flush through the mutation channel (same task or the `run`
  /// task, before this call's completion is observable host-side).
  export handle-event: async func(target: u32, name: u16, payload: payload, ev: borrow<dom-event>);
}
